    publish('/ai-tools/coding-assistant-checklist/','How to choose an AI coding assistant','A practical evaluation checklist for Cursor and GitHub Copilot.',
        '<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">BUYING GUIDE</p><h1>Which coding assistant should you pay for?</h1><p class="lede">Try one small job in your usual editor before subscribing. Keep the tool that makes the job easier to review and maintain.</p></section><article class="prose"><h2>Use a task you can verify</h2><p>Pick a known bug or add one form check. Commit the starting code, write down the expected behaviour and run the tests after the assistant finishes. Read the diff: how much did you correct, and can you explain the code you kept?</p><h2>Compare workflow before price</h2><p><a href="/ai-tools/cursor/">Cursor</a> is worth examining if you want an editor centered on agent-assisted work. <a href="/ai-tools/github-copilot/">GitHub Copilot</a> is worth examining if you want assistance within your existing editor and GitHub workflow. Confirm support for your environment on the official product pages.</p><h2>Write down your stopping rule</h2><p>Before activating paid usage, choose a spending limit and decide which tasks justify it. Check whether exceeding included usage stops work or creates another charge. Review renewal and cancellation terms. Avoid buying overlapping plans until you can name the different job each one does.</p><h2>Was it worth using?</h2><p>Keep a short log of completed tasks and time spent reviewing output. Count the review work as well as the code written. Keep changes you can test and explain. Never use production secrets as trial input.</p><p>Official facts and source links: <a href="/ai-tools/cursor/">Cursor guide</a> and <a href="/ai-tools/github-copilot/">Copilot guide</a>. This checklist is a suggested trial, not a comparison of measured productivity.</p></article>')
    publish('/ai-tools/ai-app-budget/','AI app budget: tools, hosting and model usage','Separate development subscriptions, hosting and inference costs before buying.',
        '<section class="page-hero"><p class="eyebrow">AI + HOSTING</p><h1>Your coding subscription is only part of the budget.</h1><p class="lede">Separate the cost of building an application from the cost of running it for visitors.</p></section><article class="prose"><h2>Keep separate budget lines</h2><p>List development tools, hosting, model inference, storage and domain renewal separately. A coding assistant helps you build; it does not automatically pay the model bill for your deployed app. An AI website builder may bundle some hosting, so check what is included before adding a VPS.</p><h2>Match the deployment to the workload</h2><p>For a static information site, first ask whether server hosting is needed at all. For an app that calls a hosted model, check API billing and where credentials will be stored. For a self-hosted model, investigate memory, accelerator support and concurrency rather than selecting a server from its introductory price alone.</p><h2>Plan for quiet and busy periods</h2><p><a href="/ai-tools/hugging-face-inference-endpoints/">Managed endpoints</a> offer autoscaling, but inspect the configuration and billing conditions. Check the bill for repeated requests, large uploads and retries. Set limits in the app before visitors can use it.</p><h2>Compare full terms</h2><p>Use <a href="/compare/">our VPS comparison</a> to inspect verified hosting offers. Then check renewal pricing, commitment length and cancellation. For <a href="/ai-tools/hostinger-ai-builder/">an AI builder</a>, confirm the eligible plan and any limits on generated changes. These are different purchases; a cheap server is not automatically the cheapest working solution.</p><h2>Before paying</h2><p>Put fixed fees and usage charges on separate lines. Set a spending limit and try a few typical requests. Work out what you will pay when any promotional credits end.</p><p>Use the official links in the product guides to fill in current rates; this guide supplies no price estimates.</p></article>')
